Cell["\<\
The Hodgkin\[Dash]Huxley model of the nerve impulse (1952), measured on the \
giant axon of the squid Loligo, is built from exactly such voltage-dependent \
S-curves for its sodium and potassium gates [0.1]. In their data the sodium \
conductance changed e-fold for every 4 mV, and the potassium conductance for \
every 5\[Dash]6 mV. The action potential itself is what you get when many of \
these switches act together.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"631cb684-2dfe-431a-a872-22807305e717"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["0.2 Oxygen saturation of hemoglobin", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"2e0add1f-a650-489e-b571-5319a19351d4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Hemoglobin binds oxygen cooperatively: once one of its four sites holds O\
\:2082, the others bind more easily. The result is the S-shaped oxygen \
dissociation curve, described since 1910 by the Hill equation [0.4]. For \
normal human blood the Hill exponent n = 2.7 fits well over most of the range \
[0.5], with half-saturation pressure ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["P", "50"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "86d5040b-5b05-41cc-810f-4390910b2123"],
 " of about 26.8 mmHg. Myoglobin, a single-site protein, binds without \
cooperativity and has a hyperbolic curve (n = 1) with much higher affinity."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b71625ea-697a-468e-b31f-c99ae7abf8f0"],

Cell["\<\
Photo 0.2. Structure of human hemoglobin (PDB 1GZX): four subunits, each with \
an oxygen-binding heme. Their interaction makes binding cooperative. Richard \
Wheeler (Zephyris), CC BY-SA 3.0, Wikimedia Commons.\
\>", "Caption",
 FontSize->14,
 FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->"640abbc4-95df-49b5-91d9-df532b1066d8"],

