Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["0.6 The response of a real neuron", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"0eb3d719-0daf-4b0f-89cf-4ff0c234802b"],

Cell["\<\
Neurons in the visual cortex fire faster as the contrast of a stimulus \
increases, but not linearly: the response grows slowly at low contrast, \
steeply in the middle, and saturates at high contrast. This contrast-response \
function has a sigmoidal shape and is usually fitted with the \
Naka\[Dash]Rushton (hyperbolic ratio) equation, first used for retinal data \
from fish in 1966 [0.14, 0.15]. Plotted against the logarithm of contrast, it \
is an S-curve.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f068f9ea-c65c-498d-9d00-6dc09bd872e8"],

Cell["\<\
Photo 0.6. A domestic cat. Albrecht and Hamilton measured contrast-response \
functions in the visual cortex of cat and monkey [0.15]. Alvesgaspar, CC \
BY-SA 3.0, Wikimedia Commons.\
\>", "Caption",
 FontSize->14,
 FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->"a7321736-c0fc-41e6-a0e9-05631ca598da"],

