    RowBox[{"R", "/", "Rmax"}], "c50", "n"}
  },
  GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}},
  GridBoxFrame->{"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}},
  GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{1}}, "Rows" -> {{0.5}}}]], "Text",
 AutoSpacing->False,
 ShowStringCharacters->False,
 FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",
 FontSize->10,ExpressionUUID->"970e1842-968d-407a-b7bf-62cce3b5991f"],

Cell["\<\
In every row the threshold sets where the switch happens and the slope sets \
how sharp it is: these are \[Theta] and y of one unit of Eq. (3). Living \
systems build complex behaviour by combining many such switches, many \
channels in one membrane, many genes in one network, many neurons in one \
brain. Sections 3 and 6 ask the mathematical version of that question: what \
can sums of S-shaped units represent?\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b7f01465-2bce-4c2f-8bd6-de605160717d"]
}, Open  ]],

