Cell["\<\
The functions differ in how fast they approach their limits: the logistic and \
tanh forms converge exponentially, while arctan and the algebraic sigmoid \
creep in like 1/t. For the theory below none of this matters. Only the two \
limits do.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"adb88d22-cba0-4868-8071-7c0dc9944ad6"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["2. Why S-shapes are used in neural networks", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"a4100b15-efe4-4f1b-b2a6-499902047c59"],

Cell["\<\
The first model neurons (McCulloch and Pitts, 1943; Rosenblatt's perceptron) \
fired or did not fire: their activation was the Heaviside step. A step has \
zero derivative almost everywhere, so you cannot train it by following \
gradients.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"4c8cfd16-af3b-4753-aaf0-149e485df603"],

Cell["\<\
An S-shaped function is a smooth step. It keeps the on/off behaviour for \
large inputs but has a useful derivative near the threshold, which is what \
backpropagation needs.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"df6d9381-a32e-42a4-83e9-2ace5ac3af14"],

Cell["\<\
It is bounded, so it squashes any input into a fixed range. Hornik, \
Stinchcombe and White (1989) call such functions squashing functions.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"124ef3a8-0204-4d2b-a6b2-0d1c3c104003"],

Cell["\<\
Its output between 0 and 1 can be read as a probability or a firing rate.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"65634ac8-cea2-441d-bb10-30f645ad521b"],

Cell["\<\
The logistic function has an especially cheap derivative, expressed through \
itself:\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"3f5cfb7f-a9cf-496a-bc85-4b5ba8f1b7df"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"\[Sigma]", "(", "t", ")"}], "=", 
    FractionBox["1", 
     RowBox[{"1", "+", 
      SuperscriptBox["e", 
       RowBox[{"-", "t"}]]}]]}], ",", "     ", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox["\[Sigma]", "\[Prime]"], "(", "t", ")"}], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"\[Sigma]", "(", "t", ")"}], 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"1", "-", 
       RowBox[{"\[Sigma]", "(", "t", ")"}]}], ")"}]}]}], ",", "     ", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"tanh", " ", "t"}], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"2", " ", 
      RowBox[{"\[Sigma]", "(", 
       RowBox[{"2", "t"}], ")"}]}], "-", "1"}]}]}], 
  TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormulaNumbered",ExpressionUUID->"40587f82-797e-\
4c51-9b20-8f6d15775675"],

Cell["Both identities of Eq. (2), checked:", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4c3daf15-d985-4919-8d1f-cc141f87b7b3"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["(*c.4*)\nFullSimplify[\n D[LogisticSigmoid[t], t] == LogisticSigmoid[t] (1 - LogisticSigmoid[t])]\nSimplify[TrigToExp[Tanh[t]] == 2/(1 + Exp[-2 t]) - 1]", "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[51]:=",ExpressionUUID->"1fd2fe73-432b-4657-8e77-aa872fb919fc"],

Cell[BoxData["True"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{4.0037895635624123`*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[52]=",ExpressionUUID->"de64fc8e-119e-469c-a018-a023004fd438"],

Cell[BoxData["True"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{4.00378956356398*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[53]=",ExpressionUUID->"1298e39e-9aab-4dd8-bd34-e8029516d6b9"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
So tanh, the default activation of BasicRecurrentLayer, is just a rescaled \
and shifted logistic: everything below applies to it too.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"dda9c1a6-68e8-4aca-b12e-0ab4f8f400bf"],

Cell["\<\
Steepness turns the smooth step back into the hard one. As \[Lambda] grows, \
\[Sigma](\[Lambda] t) approaches the Heaviside step, and this limit is the \
key idea in Cybenko's proof:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2a3b4dbc-9bc1-4668-bd00-c486a8496f00"],

Cell["\<\
Figure 2. Move the slider: the larger \[Lambda], the closer \
\[Sigma](\[Lambda]t) (solid) is to the step (dashed).\
\>", "Caption",
 FontSize->14,
 FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->"69bb60cb-88cd-49f1-9a53-104e52e1b375"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["(*c.5*)\nManipulate[\n Plot[{LogisticSigmoid[\[Lambda] t], UnitStep[t]}, {t, -3, 3},\n  PlotRange -> {-0.05, 1.05}, PlotStyle -> {Thick, Dashed},\n  PlotLegends -> Placed[LineLegend[{\"\[Sigma](\[Lambda]t)\", \"step\"},\n     LabelStyle -> figStyle], Below],\n  PlotLabel -> \"Figure 2. Steep sigmoid \[Rule] step\",\n  LabelStyle -> figStyle, ImageSize -> wFull],\n {{\[Lambda], 1}, 0.2, 50}]", "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[54]:=",ExpressionUUID->"76cec29c-7107-460f-9584-2bd0f3f3a0ad"],

