Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Reading Figure 3: what the arrows mean", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"126349a8-0035-4fb7-bf6e-095e539544f4"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["In everyday terms", "Subsubsection",ExpressionUUID->"ec95c32b-c0e8-4e15-be1c-514ca4469b40"],

Cell["\<\
Information flows in the direction of the arrows, from left to right: inputs, \
then hidden units, then the output. Nothing ever flows back, which is why \
this is called a feedforward network. Compare BasicRecurrentLayer, where the \
state loops back into the layer at the next time step.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"a3716119-a97e-4e0b-9723-ed7cf9ca06f0"],

Cell["\<\
Each arrow carries a number from the node at its tail to the node at its \
head, and multiplies it on the way by the weight written on it. A large \
weight means a strong influence, a negative weight pushes in the opposite \
direction, and a weight of zero is the same as having no arrow.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"9397bf3b-a6c0-4350-b4c8-8c765d71147a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Each hidden node ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["h", "j"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "29a4120c-f577-4cda-a356-f636245195af"],
 " adds up everything that arrives, adds its own offset ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Theta]", "j"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "1689cf63-d39a-407b-9d7a-b5bf2330166a"],
 " (not drawn), and passes the total through the S-curve. Its output answers \
one question: how strongly does this unit switch on for this input?"
}], "Item",ExpressionUUID->"42e844d6-b91b-43c1-b866-64bc7b4ac80c"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The output node G adds up the hidden units' answers, each scaled by its ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Alpha]", "j"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "8d3af173-616e-4cdd-a19f-e96364bd7e6c"],
 ". There is no S-curve at the output."
}], "Item",ExpressionUUID->"587cf00c-a2b3-458f-b243-1bc736cbf274"],

Cell["\<\
An analogy: think of a committee. Each hidden unit is a member who looks at \
the same facts (the inputs) with personal priorities (the weights y), forms a \
fairly yes-or-no opinion (the S-curve), and the chair (G) combines the \
opinions, trusting each member to a different degree (the weights \[Alpha]).\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"1735e9fe-c96c-4c9d-86e5-daebd6f653a1"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["In mathematical terms", "Subsubsection",ExpressionUUID->"38de4a3d-075b-400a-bb82-09e2c46e290e"],

Cell["\<\
Figure 3 is a directed acyclic graph. Each vertex is a quantity, and an edge \
from i to j says that quantity i is an argument of the function computed at \
j, with the edge label as its coefficient. Written node by node, then in \
matrix form:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7f281e23-4b14-47ca-8bcc-39547e285134"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[GridBox[{
    {
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       SubscriptBox["z", "j"], "=", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
         UnderoverscriptBox["\[Sum]", 
          RowBox[{"i", "=", "1"}], "n"], 
         RowBox[{
          SubscriptBox["y", "ji"], 
          SubscriptBox["x", "i"]}]}], "+", 
        SubscriptBox["\[Theta]", "j"]}]}], ",", "   ", 
      RowBox[{
       SubscriptBox["h", "j"], "=", 
       RowBox[{"\[Sigma]", "(", 
        SubscriptBox["z", "j"], ")"}]}], ",", "   ", 
      RowBox[{"G", "=", 
       RowBox[{
        UnderoverscriptBox["\[Sum]", 
         RowBox[{"j", "=", "1"}], "N"], 
        RowBox[{
         SubscriptBox["\[Alpha]", "j"], 
         SubscriptBox["h", "j"]}]}]}]}]},
    {
     RowBox[{"\[DoubleLongLeftRightArrow]", "   ", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"G", "(", "x", ")"}], "=", 
       RowBox[{
        SuperscriptBox["\[Alpha]", "\[Transpose]"], 
        RowBox[{"\[Sigma]", "(", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"Y", " ", "x"}], "+", "\[Theta]"}], ")"}]}]}], ",", "   ", 
      RowBox[{"Y", "\[Element]", 
       SuperscriptBox["\[DoubleStruckCapitalR]", 
        RowBox[{"N", "\[Times]", "n"}]]}]}]}
   },
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Rows" -> {{1}}}], 
  TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormulaNumbered",ExpressionUUID->"84bf7c66-5ecd-\
4c02-8f42-19c4594430fa"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The arrows of the first layer are the entries of the matrix Y. The arrow \
from ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["x", "i"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "6823dfdc-b870-4b1a-b643-e23887085994"],
 " to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["h", "j"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "1b6a3767-2d09-4862-a496-80d0619c1e99"],
 " carries ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["y", "ji"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "b57ca840-5fd5-4d8b-bc4a-da6e8a2ab1d2"],
 ": row j for the receiving unit, column i for the sending input. The arrows \
of the second layer are the entries of the vector \[Alpha]. A missing arrow \
is a zero entry."
}], "Item",ExpressionUUID->"0c1a5275-c0ec-4232-901c-7dbb71cb1c90"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The offset ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Theta]", "j"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "9ceeb268-d8c4-42d4-9abe-2242555d67d1"],
 " can be drawn as one more arrow, from a constant input that is always 1. \
Counting arrows then counts parameters: nN + N + N."
}], "Item",ExpressionUUID->"2f2d715e-5f06-420a-ad4c-fd820d8f9881"],

Cell["\<\
The graph has no cycles, so it describes a composition of functions. Eq. (4) \
is G = L\:2082 \[SmallCircle] \[Sigma] \[SmallCircle] L\:2081, with the \
affine map L\:2081(x) = Y x + \[Theta], \[Sigma] applied to each component, \
and the linear map L\:2082(h) = \[Alpha]\[CenterDot]h. This is Eq. (3) again. \
Each piece is continuous, so G is continuous (Appendix A).\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"4fab812b-9c35-4ebe-bb6d-361cc96bdbb9"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The arrows also show how derivatives flow. Each arrow's label is a local \
partial derivative (\[PartialD]",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["z", "j"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "0aec75cc-f90c-4a5e-bb7e-768a74ce4441"],
 "/\[PartialD]",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["x", "i"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "51d694ad-ef3c-4df8-962a-1e56e5bb8b8b"],
 " = ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["y", "ji"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "8d38dbfb-836c-4d0a-a382-bf3fd5558ff8"],
 " and \[PartialD]G/\[PartialD]",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["h", "j"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "7ac50473-38a6-47f0-b273-011f2ea06bc1"],
 " = ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Alpha]", "j"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "7eb3270a-f680-470b-882c-599b512a95e3"],
 "), and each node contributes \[PartialD]",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["h", "j"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "2f567bfc-2880-4265-98a4-4ad4933f5ebb"],
 "/\[PartialD]",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["z", "j"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "135741c0-8dd8-4ce9-9555-3990bc96e94c"],
 " = \[Sigma]\[Prime](",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["z", "j"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "8575a28b-03f8-4298-9bf8-f84084405549"],
 "). By the chain rule, the derivative of the output with respect to an input \
is the sum, over every path from that input to G, of the product of the local \
derivatives along the path:"
}], "Item",ExpressionUUID->"d26153b2-a9bd-41c7-b0d6-9022c79f9538"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   FractionBox[
    RowBox[{"\[PartialD]", "G"}], 
    RowBox[{"\[PartialD]", 
     SubscriptBox["x", "i"]}]], "=", 
   RowBox[{
    UnderoverscriptBox["\[Sum]", 
     RowBox[{"j", "=", "1"}], "N"], 
    RowBox[{
     UnderscriptBox[
      SubscriptBox["\[Alpha]", "j"], 
      RowBox[{"h", "\[Rule]", "G"}]], "  ", 
     UnderscriptBox[
      RowBox[{
       SuperscriptBox["\[Sigma]", "\[Prime]"], "(", 
       SubscriptBox["z", "j"], ")"}], 
      RowBox[{"inside", " ", 
       SubscriptBox["h", "j"]}]], "  ", 
     UnderscriptBox[
      SubscriptBox["y", "ji"], 
      RowBox[{"x", "\[Rule]", "h"}]]}]}]}], 
  TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormulaNumbered",ExpressionUUID->"007cbffd-1d3a-\
446a-9713-de06b67f8952"],

Cell["\<\
Computing Eq. (5) for all inputs at once, by walking the arrows backwards \
from G, is backpropagation. A numerical check against automatic \
differentiation. All its variables are local to the Module, so nothing \
defined elsewhere can interfere:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"53029b00-f940-4cf1-be80-c0bfef74cd8d"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["(*c.7*)\nModule[{Yw, th, al, gNet, x0, auto, z, pathSum, u, v},\n SeedRandom[2];\n Yw = RandomReal[{-2, 2}, {3, 2}];   (* weights y_ji *)\n th = RandomReal[{-1, 1}, 3];        (* offsets \[Theta]_j *)\n al = RandomReal[{-1, 1}, 3];        (* output weights \[Alpha]_j *)\n gNet[x1_, x2_] := al . LogisticSigmoid[Yw . {x1, x2} + th];\n x0 = {0.3, -0.7};\n auto = D[gNet[u, v], {{u, v}}] /. {u -> x0[[1]], v -> x0[[2]]};\n z = Yw . x0 + th;\n pathSum = Table[\n   Sum[al[[j]] LogisticSigmoid[z[[j]]] (1 - LogisticSigmoid[z[[j]]]) *\n     Yw[[j, i]], {j, 3}],\n   {i, 2}];\n <|\"automatic\" -> auto, \"path sum, Eq. (5)\" -> pathSum,\n  \"difference\" -> Max@Abs[auto - pathSum]|>]", "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[63]:=",ExpressionUUID->"3ab1188d-6413-4006-b9e8-7755fd21944d"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"\<\"automatic\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"0.034067618076169974`", ",", 
      RowBox[{"-", "0.027795422544044067`"}]}], "}"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"path sum, Eq. (5)\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"0.034067618076169995`", ",", 
      RowBox[{"-", "0.027795422544044074`"}]}], "}"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"difference\"\>", "\[Rule]", "2.0816681711721685`*^-17"}]}], 
  "\[RightAssociation]"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{4.003789563685416*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[64]=",ExpressionUUID->"428e0407-de12-418c-a0ac-ff5c10a10653"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The difference is at machine-precision level (about ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["10", 
    RowBox[{"-", "17"}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "05e74fd6-9d1e-4c2b-b268-57cf5bfc8c9f"],
 ")."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"56da5cd1-3f06-4830-a1f6-ae5eae80523b"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Definitions and results", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"66f9fb21-c474-4bc7-b3c5-5baa7e10ba05"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Discriminatory. ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "\[Sigma] is discriminatory if the only finite signed regular Borel measure \
\[Mu] on ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["I", "n"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "1688fe37-5885-4f03-975f-df92581663c2"],
 " that satisfies"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"826e35a9-fce9-49c2-a746-7607a04f3a64"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["\[Integral]", 
     SubscriptBox["I", "n"]], 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"\[Sigma]", "(", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        SuperscriptBox["y", "\[Transpose]"], "x"}], "+", "\[Theta]"}], ")"}], 
     
     RowBox[{"\[DifferentialD]", 
      RowBox[{"\[Mu]", "(", "x", ")"}]}]}]}], "=", "0", "   ", 
   RowBox[{"for", " ", "all"}], "   ", 
   RowBox[{"y", "\[Element]", 
    SuperscriptBox["\[DoubleStruckCapitalR]", "n"]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\[Theta]", "\[Element]", "\[DoubleStruckCapitalR]"}]}], 
  TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormulaNumbered",ExpressionUUID->"32944043-15a6-\
404e-85b3-4cb173181730"],

Cell["\<\
is \[Mu] = 0. In words, Eq. (6) says that no nonzero measure is invisible to \
every ridge function \[Sigma](y\[CenterDot]x + \[Theta]).\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8ac717c0-a548-4988-b5a4-3691e0cdb57c"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Theorem 1 (Cybenko 1989). ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "If \[Sigma] is continuous and discriminatory, the sums G(x) of Eq. (3) are \
dense in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"C", "(", 
    SubscriptBox["I", "n"], ")"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "8508532c-7044-4ad3-9d46-a3c3b326c9ed"],
 ", the space of continuous functions on the cube (Appendix A): for every \
continuous f and every \[CurlyEpsilon] > 0 there is a G with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"\[LeftBracketingBar]", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"G", "(", "x", ")"}], "-", 
      RowBox[{"f", "(", "x", ")"}]}], "\[RightBracketingBar]"}], "<", 
    "\[CurlyEpsilon]"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "6c1360e9-3712-4bb8-86db-0c2f320de08b"],
 " for all x in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["I", "n"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "402e200b-4d13-4fab-a3b2-0c9d0d26233b"],
 "."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"62a9ef1e-b401-493c-80b3-07f8c9faf6ea"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Lemma 1 (Cybenko 1989). ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "Every bounded, measurable sigmoidal function is discriminatory; in \
particular every continuous one is."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"353676e7-f172-47e3-b6ca-7627895c777c"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Theorem 2 (Cybenko 1989, the universal approximation theorem). ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "For any continuous sigmoidal \[Sigma], the sums G(x) are dense in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"C", "(", 
    SubscriptBox["I", "n"], ")"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "b9887f7f-1fa0-4419-8191-aa49d502aac8"],
 ". One hidden layer of S-shaped units (Figure 3, with N large enough) can \
approximate any continuous function on the cube as closely as you like."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d94b6015-f7c8-40f9-bcae-6dea191e2dad"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Theorem 3 (decision regions). ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "Let \[Sigma] be continuous and sigmoidal, split ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["I", "n"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "861fa3b7-4aa1-4ab4-83eb-cf064243f097"],
 " into finitely many measurable pieces ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["P", "1"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "cc3e5071-89b1-46a2-a629-a6bb6af20a0e"],
 ", \[Ellipsis], ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["P", "k"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "284b509a-e00d-41d3-b28f-e4b205099627"],
 ", and let f(x) = j on ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["P", "j"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "fac3b1ac-a3f1-41d4-8d6b-0cc83dbd1873"],
 ". For every \[CurlyEpsilon] > 0 there is a sum G of Eq. (3) and a set D \
\[Subset] ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["I", "n"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "a01e11bd-331f-4eac-80be-f4369bd61965"],
 " of measure at least 1 - \[CurlyEpsilon] with |G(x) - f(x)| < \
\[CurlyEpsilon] for all x in D. In words: a one-hidden-layer network can \
classify correctly everywhere except on a set as small as you like."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7393eb95-ddcd-4889-8a9c-9a1173999c09"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Theorem 4 (L\.b9 approximation). ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "If \[Sigma] is bounded, measurable and sigmoidal, the step included, the \
sums G of Eq. (3) are dense in L\.b9(",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["I", "n"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "124fd96e-3304-4756-8245-c40a461a0839"],
 "): the area between f and G can be made as small as you like."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"1f74319a-1d5b-43ab-bd72-95749982c035"],

Cell["Theorems 1\[Dash]4 and Lemma 1 are numbered as in Cybenko's paper.", \
"Text",ExpressionUUID->"70f169b7-6694-4677-8d17-c0dd92156c52"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["How the proof works", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"513428d5-64d0-4c3f-9c9d-64d266aa5fb5"],

Cell["\<\
Theorem 1 is pure functional analysis and never builds a network:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d0db33d5-2cd7-472f-a67a-caa61c2ef881"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The set S of all sums G is a linear subspace of C(",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["I", "n"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "4bcc2625-8ef7-4987-b698-cad10cd0f1a2"],
 "). Suppose its closure R is not all of C(",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["I", "n"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "20c5a59e-5085-44a5-8013-e8aa8356d22b"],
 ")."
}], "ItemNumbered",ExpressionUUID->"63be9617-bebe-4fe3-a580-34f4fd0e540b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Hahn\[Dash]Banach then gives a nonzero bounded linear functional L on C(",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["I", "n"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "b7228d73-03a0-4872-97fc-de001d2d93ea"],
 ") that vanishes on R."
}], "ItemNumbered",ExpressionUUID->"0aeb11a3-a046-434b-8188-6c29fc951a7a"],

Cell["\<\
The Riesz representation theorem writes L(h) = \[Integral] h d\[Mu] for some \
measure \[Mu] \[NotEqual] 0.\
\>", "ItemNumbered",ExpressionUUID->"9bef2eaf-d246-49cd-ad48-c3cb4a4f9557"],

Cell["\<\
Every \[Sigma](y\[CenterDot]x + \[Theta]) lies in R, so its integral against \
\[Mu] is 0. Since \[Sigma] is discriminatory (Eq. (6)), \[Mu] = 0, a \
contradiction.\
\>", "ItemNumbered",ExpressionUUID->"4a093ff2-696d-425f-94ac-6fb1c0c11a43"],

Cell["\<\
Lemma 1 is where the S-shape enters. Scaling the argument, \
\[Sigma](\[Lambda](y\[CenterDot]x + \[Theta]) + \[CurlyPhi]) with \[Lambda] \
\[Rule] \[Infinity] converges boundedly to a step: 1 on one half-space, 0 on \
the other (Figure 2). So \[Mu] would have to give zero mass to every \
half-space. That makes every one-dimensional projection of \[Mu] zero, hence \
its Fourier transform vanishes, hence \[Mu] = 0.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4f7d8004-8010-4915-9a20-dbec3490a9a9"],

Cell["\<\
Note what the theorem does not say. It is an existence result: it gives no \
formula for the weights and no bound on N. Cybenko's closing remarks name the \
size of N as the open question, and he suspects that most problems will need \
\"astronomical numbers of terms\" because of the curse of dimensionality. \
Barron (1993) later gave rates for functions with a bounded Fourier moment.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d7866f9d-6986-4b1c-bc98-4d07ab4fa539"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["4. Seeing it work in one dimension: staircases of S-shapes", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"d17b6a01-6d5c-4bed-9eda-aafa38005179"],

Cell["\<\
The proof idea can be made constructive in 1D. Steep sigmoids are nearly \
steps, and a sum of steps is a staircase that follows f. Place a step halfway \
between consecutive grid points k/N, with height equal to the change of f \
there:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"04c164d1-683b-43c8-b982-1130ecf8f02e"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["G", "N"], "(", "x", ")"}], "=", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"f", "(", "0", ")"}], "+", 
    RowBox[{
     UnderoverscriptBox["\[Sum]", 
      RowBox[{"k", "=", "1"}], "N"], 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"(", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"f", "(", 
         FractionBox["k", "N"], ")"}], "-", 
        RowBox[{"f", "(", 
         FractionBox[
          RowBox[{"k", "-", "1"}], "N"], ")"}]}], ")"}], 
      RowBox[{"\[Sigma]", "(", 
       RowBox[{"\[Lambda]", 
        RowBox[{"(", 
         RowBox[{"x", "-", 
          FractionBox[
           RowBox[{"k", "-", 
            RowBox[{"1", "/", "2"}]}], "N"]}], ")"}]}], ")"}]}]}]}]}], 
  TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormulaNumbered",ExpressionUUID->"ee70a18c-c8a0-\
40d2-9d63-e5579f228cff"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Eq. (7) is a special case of Eq. (3), with n = 1, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["y", "k"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "75803712-0fd5-4e80-ad8a-9006d51eb406"],
 " = \[Lambda], ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Theta]", "k"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "2e26607f-7524-4821-addd-8e727a593923"],
 " = -\[Lambda](k - 1/2)/N, and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Alpha]", "k"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "957163ee-4dc8-41f0-a686-31281c34fd5d"],
 " equal to the jumps of f, plus a constant."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"09c352bb-d8f2-4ecc-8df4-7f409580eaf7"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Example A: a smooth function", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"8f603d95-200c-463a-baca-3a3949f532c2"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Figure 4. The function f (blue) and the staircase ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["G", "10"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "e66c6c91-39ae-4f95-baa5-183fa18c123a"],
 " of Eq. (7) built from 10 steep sigmoids (orange)."
}], "Caption",
 FontSize->14,
 FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->"f4a001bb-b624-4aa8-8c17-1191ea52a751"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["(*c.8*)\nf[x_] := Sin[2 Pi x] + x^2/2;\nstair[fun_, n_, \[Lambda]_][x_] := fun[0] +\n   Sum[(fun[k/n] - fun[(k - 1)/n]) *\n     LogisticSigmoid[\[Lambda] (x - (k - 1/2)/n)], {k, 1, n}];\nPlot[{f[x], stair[f, 10, 200][x]}, {x, 0, 1},\n PlotLegends -> Placed[LineLegend[{\"f\", Subscript[\"G\", 10]},\n    LabelStyle -> figStyle], Below],\n PlotLabel -> \"Figure 4. Example A, N = 10\", LabelStyle -> figStyle,\n ImageSize -> wFull]", "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[65]:=",ExpressionUUID->"8036e33a-5f4b-4cec-b13c-37d4850ea54b"],

