Cell["What a test run showed, and why:", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7f639b8f-ed3b-4180-9683-fadb5b5f6f29"],

Cell["\<\
A and B: the error halves when N doubles. A kink costs nothing, because the \
staircase only needs the values of f, not its derivative.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"969b3e65-5e1c-4546-9468-94c6ca256db9"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "C: the error shrinks only by a factor of about 1/\[Sqrt]2 per doubling \
(0.24, 0.17, 0.12, 0.086, 0.060), so it is O(",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["N", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"-", "1"}], "/", "2"}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "f9c2c301-c19e-494d-8452-57c4e9998a68"],
 "). Near 0, \[Sqrt]x changes by \[Sqrt](1/N) over an interval of length \
1/N."
}], "Item",ExpressionUUID->"a8f771d6-75fe-4182-86df-f7e6ef672149"],

Cell["\<\
D: the error is large (0.86) until the steps are finer than the bump, then \
falls like 1/N. A function with fine detail needs many units before the \
approximation starts to work.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"7f8a2314-c552-486e-9261-25f7736e99d7"],

Cell["\<\
E: the error never goes to zero; it stays between 0.5 and 1. A jump cannot be \
followed uniformly by a continuous function, because every finite sum of \
sigmoids is continuous. In the weaker sense of area between the curves, \
Theorem 4, the error does go to zero.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"51f0eb67-9d2b-4f67-a47f-446e6c076c82"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The rule behind all five: the error is of the order of the largest change \
of f over an interval of length 1/N. That quantity is the modulus of \
continuity ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Omega]", "f"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "56deea8d-08db-4a49-976e-5de8dea7e32d"],
 "(1/N), Eq. (12) in Appendix A. It goes to 0 exactly when f is uniformly \
continuous, and Table 6 computes it for these examples."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"6c5c1548-007b-4116-a4ad-27be105a028d"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
5. Seeing it work in two dimensions: fitting the outer weights\
\>", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"cf0f537e-f89a-4f10-ad9e-5cef0f312c03"],

Cell["\<\
The target is a smooth function of two variables. First, look at it:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"aca98d19-01ae-4911-b76b-8dce80e6ee35"],

Cell["Figure 6. The target g(x, y) = sin(\[Pi]x) cos(\[Pi]y) on [-1, \
1]\.b2.", "Caption",
 FontSize->14,
 FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->"c0ce23cc-9038-448e-8c12-9ec0a9a7d096"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["(*c.12*)\ng[x_, y_] := Sin[Pi x] Cos[Pi y];\nPlot3D[g[x, y], {x, -1, 1}, {y, -1, 1},\n AxesLabel -> {\"x\", \"y\", \"g\"}, ColorFunction -> \"TemperatureMap\",\n PlotLabel -> \"Figure 6. Target g(x, y)\", LabelStyle -> figStyle,\n ImageSize -> 0.8 wFull]", "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[79]:=",ExpressionUUID->"6560fcf5-7c97-40b8-b308-cacdd9fd4771"],

