 CellLabel->"Out[91]=",ExpressionUUID->"c7b27eb8-d529-4e2c-bbf7-437bd537dcc3"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["6. The original: Kolmogorov's superposition theorem (1957)", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"ea4e5b3c-5f45-41a8-86bd-2cf6cf49d669"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Background: Hilbert's 13th problem", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"65c8ef03-c46c-4147-ad85-b13d54df4c58"],

Cell["\<\
In 1900 Hilbert asked whether the roots of the degree-7 equation \
x\:2077 + a x\.b3 + b x\.b2 + c x + 1 = 0, a function of three \
variables (a, b, c), can be written as a composition of functions of only two \
variables. He conjectured that they cannot. The continuous version of the \
question was settled in the opposite direction:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"987803ac-ccc1-41bd-9bf7-33f87640cb52"],

Cell["\<\
1956: Kolmogorov shows that every continuous function of several variables is \
a finite superposition of continuous functions of three variables.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"1302f557-f24f-46c8-8efc-da8f2fe875f2"],

Cell["\<\
1957: Arnold, then 19 and Kolmogorov's student, reduces this to functions of \
two variables, answering Hilbert's question for continuous functions.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"7b581521-c329-47b8-ac58-43106d9f12a1"],

Cell["\<\
1957: Kolmogorov goes all the way down: functions of one variable plus \
addition are enough.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"b25f109f-06c0-4961-a3cb-931f7a6800b3"],

Cell["The algebraic version of Hilbert's problem is still open.", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"11bc6da2-d43d-4de9-a3f3-51fe575c306d"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The theorem", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"23ce0dbd-4fe2-4b3d-8e56-abb3a6b202b0"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Theorem 5 (Kolmogorov 1957). ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "Every continuous (Appendix A) ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"f", ":", 
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox[
      RowBox[{"[", 
       RowBox[{"0", ",", "1"}], "]"}], "n"], "\[Rule]", 
     "\[DoubleStruckCapitalR]"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "b16593d0-f0cb-459c-affb-55c9c25d5621"],
 ", n \[GreaterEqual] 2, can be written exactly as"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"1f08feab-6068-4a8e-acae-a834e74c6e84"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"f", "(", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["x", "1"], ",", "\[Ellipsis]", ",", 
     SubscriptBox["x", "n"]}], ")"}], "=", 
   RowBox[{
    UnderoverscriptBox["\[Sum]", 
     RowBox[{"q", "=", "0"}], 
     RowBox[{"2", "n"}]], 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["\[CapitalPhi]", "q"], 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{
       UnderoverscriptBox["\[Sum]", 
        RowBox[{"p", "=", "1"}], "n"], 
       RowBox[{
        SubscriptBox["\[Psi]", 
         RowBox[{"p", ",", "q"}]], "(", 
        SubscriptBox["x", "p"], ")"}]}], ")"}]}]}]}], 
  TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormulaNumbered",ExpressionUUID->"84a56499-5262-\
4d99-8bfa-b1da877c18f9"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The inner functions ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Psi]", 
    RowBox[{"p", ",", "q"}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "62cf6210-04cc-4cdc-8292-bec304fa10be"],
 " of Eq. (8) are continuous and increasing, and universal: they depend only \
on n, not on f."
}], "Item",ExpressionUUID->"c4cbf37c-f4e2-44d7-94f3-b200ab7ca772"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The outer functions ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[CapitalPhi]", "q"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "f2a41f5f-86fd-4347-8b3e-3233875711c5"],
 " are continuous and depend on f."
}], "Item",ExpressionUUID->"52f23045-c62b-4240-8d73-6c3d735c8342"],

Cell["\<\
The number of terms is fixed at 2n + 1, and the representation is exact, not \
approximate.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"6f43193c-4a9c-47c7-99d5-6c6de9916fdd"],

Cell["\<\
Later refinements by Lorentz and Sprecher reduce the outer functions to a \
single function, and the inner functions to scaled and shifted copies of one \
function.\
\>", "Item",ExpressionUUID->"84e9935e-b651-43b7-a8a6-3fef1ca05107"],

Cell["\<\
The message: the only genuinely multivariate function you ever need is \
addition.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"033a446d-8054-4703-98ef-215c47fb31e7"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Elementary examples of the idea", "Subsubsection",ExpressionUUID->"df1e1c10-a17d-434d-84bb-84fc1363f30c"],

Cell["\<\
Multiplication, the simplest nonlinear function of two variables, is a sum of \
one-variable functions of sums:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c94ff8ff-a8d4-4d60-92bd-3d75d3a9ad4e"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
(*c.16*)
FullSimplify[((x + y)^2 - (x - y)^2)/4 == x y]
FullSimplify[Exp[Log[x] + Log[y]] == x y, x > 0 && y > 0]\
\>", "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[92]:=",ExpressionUUID->"e3e0282d-a5cb-4f07-9436-86530104f313"],

Cell[BoxData["True"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{4.003789574841558*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[93]=",ExpressionUUID->"0a0cfa0f-fec6-4adf-8054-59df41120fb2"],

Cell[BoxData["True"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{4.0037895748427477`*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[94]=",ExpressionUUID->"a035f2ac-75ab-4a55-bff8-d163d56728b1"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The second identity already has the shape of Eq. (8): inner functions \[Psi] \
= Log on each variable, add them, then one outer function \[CapitalPhi] = Exp.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"706512dd-06b6-4231-8145-4e69a16294e8"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["As a network", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"789b951c-c591-4e3b-a1f5-cd094a53326f"],

Cell["\<\
Hecht-Nielsen (1987) pointed out that Eq. (8) is a feedforward network: one \
layer computes the inner functions, the next adds them and applies the outer \
functions, and the output adds those. For n = 2 there are 2n + 1 = 5 branches \
(Figure 8).\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f252c138-3503-4a7f-b8ab-7cc62ef08ad4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Figure 8. Eq. (8) as a network for n = 2. The arrows mean something \
slightly different from those in Figure 3: they carry a value without \
multiplying it by a weight. Each ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Psi]", "pq"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "2ed1a472-5609-4928-9969-ef914a9f2e50"],
 " node applies its own one-variable function to its input, each ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[CapitalPhi]", "q"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "de48b5f2-5ae5-451d-8e81-bb17d8f4188c"],
 " node adds what arrives and then applies ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[CapitalPhi]", "q"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "41133b56-524c-495a-945e-a8ab7b0d2407"],
 ", and f adds the five branches. In Figure 3 every node applies the same \
\[Sigma] and all the flexibility sits in the weights on the arrows; here \
there are no weights and all the flexibility sits in the node functions."
}], "Caption",
 FontSize->14,
 FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->"f5755381-88c8-4ace-a21f-86dba7ae8d1b"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["(*c.17*)\nlay = {\"LayeredDigraphEmbedding\", \"Orientation\" -> Left};\nGraph[Flatten@{\n   Table[\"x\" <> ToString[p] ->\n     Subscript[\"\[Psi]\", ToString[p] <> ToString[q]], {p, 2}, {q, 0, 4}],\n   Table[Subscript[\"\[Psi]\", ToString[p] <> ToString[q]] ->\n     Subscript[\"\[CapitalPhi]\", q], {p, 2}, {q, 0, 4}],\n   Table[Subscript[\"\[CapitalPhi]\", q] -> \"f\", {q, 0, 4}]},\n GraphLayout -> lay, VertexLabels -> Placed[\"Name\", Above],\n VertexLabelStyle -> Directive[Black, figFont], ImageSize -> wFull,\n PlotLabel -> Style[\"Figure 8. Kolmogorov network, n = 2\", figFont]]", "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[95]:=",ExpressionUUID->"b2a115a7-44b1-4941-92a4-5c15f350ee06"],

